Here's how to handle each scenario in MSTest:

## 1. Windows-only tests (platform filtering)

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[TestCategory("WindowsOnly")]
// MSTest v3+ supports conditional skip via OSCondition
[SupportedOSPlatform("windows")]
public void Registry_ReadsExpectedValue()
{
    if (!OperatingSystem.IsWindows())
    {
        Assert.Inconclusive("Test requires Windows.");
        return;
    }

    // Windows registry code here
}
```

Or use a **custom attribute** for reuse:

```csharp
public class WindowsOnlyAttribute : TestMethodAttribute
{
    public override TestResult[] Execute(ITestMethod testMethod)
    {
        if (!OperatingSystem.IsWindows())
            return new[] { new TestResult { Outcome = UnitTestOutcome.Inconclusive,
                TestFailureException = new AssertInconclusiveException("Requires Windows.") } };
        return base.Execute(testMethod);
    }
}
```

## 2. Skip in CI (environment-based filtering)

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[TestCategory("RequiresGPU")]
public void Render_ProducesExpectedOutput()
{
    if (Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("CI") == "true")
    {
        Assert.Inconclusive("Skipped in CI — requires local GPU.");
        return;
    }

    // GPU test logic
}
```

Then optionally filter at the runner level:

```bash
dotnet test --filter "TestCategory!=RequiresGPU"
```

## 3. Flaky/retry logic (external API)

```csharp
// Custom retry attribute
public class RetryAttribute : TestMethodAttribute
{
    private readonly int _maxRetries;
    public RetryAttribute(int maxRetries = 3) => _maxRetries = maxRetries;

    public override TestResult[] Execute(ITestMethod testMethod)
    {
        TestResult[] results = null;
        for (int i = 0; i < _maxRetries; i++)
        {
            results = base.Execute(testMethod);
            if (results.All(r => r.Outcome == UnitTestOutcome.Passed))
                return results;
        }
        return results; // return last failure
    }
}

[Retry(3)]
public void WeatherApi_ReturnsForecast()
{
    var result = _weatherClient.GetForecast("Seattle");
    Assert.IsNotNull(result);
}
```

## 4. Cleanup even on failure (use `[TestCleanup]` or `try/finally`)

```csharp
[TestClass]
public class TempFileTests
{
    private string _tempFile;

    [TestInitialize]
    public void Setup()
    {
        _tempFile = Path.GetTempFileName();
    }

    [TestCleanup] // Runs even if the test throws
    public void Cleanup()
    {
        if (File.Exists(_tempFile))
            File.Delete(_tempFile);
    }

    [TestMethod]
    public void ProcessFile_WritesExpectedContent()
    {
        File.WriteAllText(_tempFile, "test data");
        var result = MyProcessor.Process(_tempFile);
        Assert.AreEqual("expected", result);
    }
}
```

---

**Summary table:**

| Scenario | Approach |
|----------|----------|
| Platform-specific | `Assert.Inconclusive()` guard or custom `TestMethodAttribute` |
| Skip in CI | Environment variable check + `TestCategory` for filter |
| Flaky tests | Custom `RetryAttribute` overriding `Execute` |
| Guaranteed cleanup | `[TestCleanup]` (always runs, even on failure) |

`[TestCleanup]` is the MSTest equivalent of `try/finally` at the test level — it's the idiomatic choice for resource cleanup.
